<section class="card" aria-labelledby="h-el">
  <div class="card-head">
    <h2 id="h-el"><span class="step">4</span> Add elements <span class="opt">optional</span></h2>
    <p>Juice or syrup, added after filtering or during the steep. Add as many as you like.</p>
  </div>

  <div id="elements" class="elements"></div>
  <p class="note" id="elements-empty">No elements yet. The cold brew stays plain.</p>
  <button type="button" class="btn add" id="btn-add-element">+ Add element</button>

  <div class="serve" id="serve-wrap" hidden>
    <label class="f" for="serve">Cold brew per serving
      <span class="box anchor"><input id="serve" type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="10" min="0" /><span class="unit">ml</span></span>
    </label>
    <div class="chips" role="group" aria-label="Serving presets">
      <button type="button" class="chip" data-serve="120">120 ml · over ice</button>
      <button type="button" class="chip" data-serve="200">200 ml</button>
      <button type="button" class="chip" data-serve="250">250 ml · bottle</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <details class="research">
    <summary>After filtering vs during the steep</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p><strong>Short answer: add after filtering.</strong> You can taste each glass and adjust, and the cold brew stays a clean base you can reuse.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>No SCA guideline covers adding fruit to a cold brew steep. SCA's "co-fermented" describes farm processing, not brewing.</li>
        <li>Cold brew is low-acid (pH around 5), well above the pH 4.6 food-safety line. Fruit acid doesn't bring it below that.</li>
        <li>Fruit or syrup in a 12–14 hour steep adds sugar and microbes to that low-acid liquid. Keep it refrigerated the whole time. My rule of thumb, not a tested shelf life: drink it within 3 days.</li>
        <li>Juice or syrup added during the steep counts as liquid, so it dilutes the brew. The calculator adds it into the effective ratio and yield.</li>
        <li>Whole fruit (grams) both soaks up and releases liquid, so its effect on yield can't be predicted. Measure your yield after the first batch.</li>
        <li>Once it's in, you can't dial it back. Treat during-steep elements as an experiment until you've found a winner after filtering.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="src">Sources: <a href="https://blogs.cornell.edu/fvc/2017/09/20/cold-brew-coffee/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cornell Food Venture Center, cold brew safety</a> · <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jfs.70024" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Singh et al., pH and temperature on pathogens in cold brew (2025)</a> · <a href="https://www.bccdc.ca/resource-gallery/Documents/Educational%20Materials/EH/FPS/Food/Nitro_Cold_Brew_Coffee_Food_Safety_Risks.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BCCDC, cold brew food safety risks</a></p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

